<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:aapt="http://schemas.android.com/aapt"
    android:width="24dp" android:height="24dp"
    android:viewportWidth="24" android:viewportHeight="24">
    <!-- Badge for media server connection rows. **Deliberately does not follow either official logo** — Jellyfin's three converging fins and Emby's rounded e are exactly the trademark-protected parts; the closer the resemblance, the higher the risk (the test is "likelihood of confusion"), and non-free artwork would not pass the libre / F-Droid gate either. So the shape is a generic play key, and recognition is left entirely to **color**: Jellyfin explicitly states that color is not part of trademark protection, and the purple-blue gradient is free to use on someone else's own shape to indicate belonging to that ecosystem; Emby uses its official green, and a single color likewise does not constitute a trademark.
         ★ You **cannot write android:tint** here — tint would completely cover the gradient below, leaving only a flat color.
         The shape is an **equilateral** triangle (all three angles 60°) with rounded corners: each side retreats by r/tan(θ/2) and is joined with a circular arc.
         Coordinates were back-solved (post-rounding bounding box 3.51,2.5 - 20.49,21.5, fills the canvas with a 2.5 margin) — editing coordinates directly would break the rounded corner joins and misalign the gradient endpoints. -->
    <path android:pathData="M6.51,2.77 L19.49,10.27 A2.0,2.0 0 0 1 19.49,13.73 L6.51,21.23 A2.0,2.0 0 0 1 3.51,19.50 L3.51,4.50 A2.0,2.0 0 0 1 6.51,2.77 Z">
        <aapt:attr name="android:fillColor">
            <gradient android:type="linear"
                android:startX="3.51" android:startY="21.5"
                android:endX="20.49" android:endY="2.5"
                android:startColor="#AA5CC3" android:endColor="#00A4DC" />
        </aapt:attr>
    </path>
</vector>
